      "id": "lpic2-smart-short-diagnosis",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-2",
      "unit": "linux-ata-smart-maintenance",
      "topic": "ATA storage diagnostics and monitoring",
      "title": "Run a fresh self-test on the reported ATA disk",
      "summary": "Find the disk on the maintenance ticket, run a fresh short test and record what happened.",
      "objective": "Identify the supplied disk by its ticket serial and run its short self-test to completion. Record the latest self-test status and its first-error LBA if it reports a read failure. Keep the other supplied disk's test history untouched.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 18,
      "retry_guidance": "Use the failed requirement and the latest output or selected preview. Repair the specific mismatch and check again.",
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Try the separate example",
        "Complete the independent assignment"
      ],
      "guide": [
        {
          "title": "Understand the task",
          "why": "A Linux device path can change when devices are detected in a different order. Use smartctl -i on a listed path and match Serial Number to the ticket before starting any test.",
          "action": "Read the worked example.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Investigate with separate inputs",
          "why": "The overall health result from smartctl -H is a current headline. The self-test log tells what individual tests observed, with the newest stored result first. A drive can report PASSED while its log still contains a read failure.",
          "action": "Compare the guided practice output and supplied device facts.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Complete the independent assignment",
          "why": "The current device identities and outcomes determine credit.",
          "action": "Check the independent task after making changes.",
          "commands": []
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The selected disk completed a fresh short test. The ticket matches that test, and the other disk's history stayed intact. Treat a read failure as evidence to investigate. A SMART test does not repair damage or guarantee future health.",
      "hints": [
        "Run smartctl -i on each listed path and compare Serial Number to the ticket.",
        "The start command does not finish the test. Use the virtual time button until the test has completed.",
        "Compare the newest self-test line against the ticket status and first-error LBA. Check the line number and lifetime hours as well as the words."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-201-202-objectives/",
        "https://github.com/smartmontools/smartmontools/blob/main/src/smartctl.8.in",
        "https://www.smartmontools.org/static/doxygen/smartd_8cpp_source.html",
        "https://github.com/mirror/smartmontools/blob/master/smartd.conf.5.in"
      ],
      "simplifications": "The supplied virtual ATA drives model disk identity, overall health, a bounded short test and its self-test log. The saved smartd.conf subset previews explicit device rules in two supplied layouts. No host device access, kernel or service changes, physical timings, extended tests, arbitrary shell, device scans, SSH, actual SMART commands, alerts or hardware monitoring.",
      "prerequisites": [
        "lpic2-mount-identity"
      ],
